the scandal/powergrab. I'll try to find the Reason article, but it will
probably be a month or so before it reaches the top of my delayable-todo
list.
    The reasons thalidomide passed the tests is simple, the fetal damage it
does only occurs in humans deficiant in one specific vitamin. (I wish I
could remeber which one. I think it was C.) At the time of the Campbell
editorial, it hadn't been replicated in _any_ animal study, with or without
malnutrition. The Reason article, a decade later, stated there still was no
non-human test that would catch thalidomide. The only known strategy to
prevent being caught by another thalidomide is to let people in other
countrys use the drugs long eanough to see the effects before allowing them
to be used here.  It would be political suicide to openly advocate that,
but an unnesessarily long aproval procedure can be justified on other
grounds.
