   Why is there a commons?  Are you asserting that the mere existence of a
   species is a good enjoyed by a class of people who cannot be excluded from
   that benefit?  What good do they so enjoy?  

   Again, what sort of value are you speaking about?  Why should I care about
   the species apart from its individual members?  Please explicate in a
   manner that does not ascribe moral value to species qua species.

Well, I could probably dig up some environmentalist propagands and
quote it, but it isn't worth the trouble.  I will just note that a
large enough percentage of the people in this country ascribe value to
the existence of vertebrate species (whether qua species, or due to
the fear that they will turn out to be valuable later on, I don't
know) that the US has a large number of very strict laws whose
intention is to protect the continued existence of species, rather
than to protect the continued existence of individuals per se.

Attempting to replicate this sort of legal protection seems to be
rather a challenge to polycentric law, because of the particularly
thorny tragedy of the commons features of the situation.  Indeed, to
the degree that the world is a polycentric legal system in practice,
the endangered species protections have loopholes, which many people
work on eliminating.

One can "solve" this problem by simply arguing that no such laws are
necessary, but this would leave a "deficiency" in the theory of
polycentric law that might be used to argue against it.

