Date: Fri, 19 Mar 93 15:03:14 -0500
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: BRAIN: Creatures of infinite habit

Peter McCluskey writes:

> I don't believe that there is a single plasticity that is safe for a
>being to accept. I think that certain brain functions need to have the
>stability of an adult mind in order for a person to take responsibilty
>for his actions and to keep susceptibilty to dangerous memes from
>endangering him.

I agree.

>
>>  Assuming also that we want to remain as qualitatively human
>>  minds, we must prepare to deal with this handicap.
>
> The only way to remain qualitatively human is to accept stagnation as in
>choice 1. Given the near inevitability of some intelligent life-forms evolving
>beyond humanity, this goal is equivalent to choosing inferiority.

What I mean by 'remaining qualitatively human' is 'retaining the fundamental
brain dynamics that define the basis of our means of thinking'.  We can become
quantitatively super-human (more brain capacity, faster thought, new senses,
etc.), but we cannot change the fundamentals (whatever they may be - perhaps
learning through a perception-action cycle, perhaps the progression from
plasticity to stability...) without redesigning the system from scratch.  Thus
uploaders and any already existing identities are limited to quantitative
improvements only.  To use the computer analogy (Argh): if you wanted to change
a computer system from a digital representation to an analog one, you'd pretty
much have to start from scratch.  Sure, in the future (far future), we may
understand the functional components of a 'consciousness', and if these are not
dependent upon the fundamental brain dynamics (which I believe they are) then
we could transfer a 'post-human' mind to one radically 'un-human' without
'killing' it.  But this sort of speculation is premature.

This is similar to discussing time travel - we don't know that it is
definitely impossible, but physics experts doubt it very much.  Of course,
brain science is not as mature as physics is, but perhaps some of our
theories are not completely false.

>I suggest that uploaded
>brains will grow by having new "neurons" added to them, with initial
>plasticity comparable to an infant, and that the plasticity would decrease
>to that of an adult human brain as the new "modules" prove useful.

This is a good suggestion.  Along with the 'neurons' though, new function must
also be added (i.e. new target 'tissue') or else the new ones will lose in
competition with the already established and coherently firing old neurons.
Perhaps an entire sensory-cognitive-motor loop is necessary...

Thus, we may want to implant redundant "cognitive areas" (frontal cortex?) to
allow a competition between a young-type and old-type personality.  If the
environment demands plasticity, then the young personality should eventually
overtake the old.  To Extropian-minded individuals, such schizophrenic
personality competition between non-discrete identities would not sound
distasteful.


-Paul

BTW - I'm not pessimistic about our ability to understand the brain, or I
wouldn't devote so much time to studying it.  My 'pessimism' is with what is
ultimately possible.  There are tradeoffs in brain mechanics comparable to
time/space tradeoffs, velocity-accuracy/position-accuracy tradeoffs etc. and
these might spell trouble for some Extropian goals.


