Date: Fri, 19 Mar 93 16:46:11 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: ECON: Tragedy of the commons 

   From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@snark.shearson.com>

   Perhaps I'm just hopelessly thick, or prehaps as you seem to do all
   the time you are just baiting us to get a reaction, but it would seem
   to me to be crystal clear that the survival of the members of a
   species also implies the existance of the species, or, perhaps, would
   you have us believe that a species is some sort of platonic ideal that
   exists out in the world of thought and cannot be preserved merely by
   members of the species?

The trick is that the survival of a species depends on the survival of
*some* members of the species, but not on the survival of any
particular member of the species.  Thus, if you have 5 cheetahs and I
have 5 cheetahs, and 5 are sufficient to "preserve the species", it
doesn't matter to each of us which of our sets of cheetahs is
preserved.  (In fact, whichever of us disposes of ours first wins,
since the other person will then have to preserve his, and both will
get the "benefit" of cheetahs still living.)

It gets even messier when you deal with free-living species, since one
can clearly eliminate any small part of the species' habitat without
loss, but one can't combine all those eliminations.  It's also tricky
to decide what the "minimum sufficient habitat" is.

In a sense, it's because living things are crypto-anarchist objects:
They carry information that we can't (yet) get at directly, so in
order to preserve the information, we have to preserve enough of the
vessles containing the information to make sure that we don't lose it.
But no one vessle is itself valuable, or even sufficient.

Dale

