From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: organizations

   From: cappello%cs@hub.ucsb.edu (Peter Cappello)

   >Improved computers and communications can reduce the costs of these
   >dynamic institutions, but the amount of human effort needed to
   >establish the relationships needed seems to put a lower bound on the
   >transaction cost of assembling an ad-hoc organization.

   Are you are referring to the cost of figuring out, and writing, a  
   contract---a specification of what you want done? 

No, more to the cost of learining how do deal effectively with the
other people and organizations in the ad-hoc organization.

It's a little strange, but as I get more involved in business, I
realize that *very little* of business relationships can be captured
in a contract.  The situation where I go into a store and pay $1.49
for a can of beans in a strictly this-for-that trade is very unusual.
(Actually, even grocery stores aren't like that -- there is a whole
implied structure of reputation, consumer protection bureaus, and
health regulatory agencies that we depend on so much (and works so
well) that we rarely recognize that we expect them to be there.)

Real business is about *relationships*, based on the fact that I know
and can trust the people I'm dealing with.  This is crucial, because
if a transaction has any complexity at all, there are many aspects
that simply cannot be captured in a contract -- I have to rely on the
other people being "reasonable" according to the standards of the
business I'm in.  That's why introductions and "networking" contacts
are so important -- if friend A introduces B to me, I know that A has
some interest in preventing a breakdown in the relationship between me
and B.

Pop-business books have been emphasizing this in recent years, saying
that one should be careful to avoid buying by price alone, and rather
work on building up long-term and mutually trusting relationships with
one's trading partners.

When assembling an ad-hoc organization, there is a certain overhead
while everyone learns how to deal with everyone else.  This can be
reduced if all the participants are drawn from a constricted
sub-culture that has common mores and efficient circulation of
reputation information.

