Date: Fri, 19 Mar 93 17:53:00 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: organizations

   From: cappello%cs@hub.ucsb.edu (Peter Cappello)

   >Improved computers and communications can reduce the costs of these
   >dynamic institutions, but the amount of human effort needed to
   >establish the relationships needed seems to put a lower bound on the
   >transaction cost of assembling an ad-hoc organization.

   Are you are referring to the cost of figuring out, and writing, a  
   contract---a specification of what you want done? 

This seems to touch on one of the "problems" of libertarianism --
libertarians view society as a network of *rules*, whereas society is
really a nework of *relationships*.

(By "problem" I don't necessarily mean that libertarians are wrong,
but rather they they have an approach which is different from the
approach the bulk of the people in the world, and different from the
way that the world presently works.  To implement libertarianism, some
way to change the world will have to be found.)

(By "of libertarianism", I don't mean that this "problem" is
explicitly part of libertarian theory, but as an attitude, it is shot
through libertarian discussion, and seems to be held by many
libertarians.)

Peter Cappello exhibits this attitude above, by identifying the
establishment of a business relationship with the writing of a
contract.  Generally this is not so; the contract is one of the
simplest parts of a business relationship.

Henry Shapiro (sp?) once exhibited this attitude by asking (more or
less) "What changes to the list rules could we make to reduce the
bitterness and flaming?"  The trouble is, you can't make a pleasant
and harmonious society out of disputacious people by applying a set of
rules to them -- you can only make a harmonious society out of
people who *want* to be harmonious.

Much of the present world political and social system is based on a
quasi-feudal system of reciprocal obligations, where long-term
maintenance of relationships is considered more important than the
justice of any particular interaction.  Libertarianism seems to
involve replacing these relationships with a set of unambiguous and
explicit rules (voluntarily acceeded to).

It has often been noted that the countries that are governed the worst
are those with the most laws.  The US, for instance, has a less
effective legal system than most other industrialized countries, even
though it has more laws.  What people tend to overlook is that this is
not because laws are evil or disruptive per se, but rather that they
are a poor substitute for relationships.  If you want a country to run
smoothly, you have to imbue everybody with the sense that it is
important for them to work hard to make it run smoothly, as is done in
Japan.  As soon as each person feels that it is acceptable to damage
his relationship with another person simply because it is profitable
for him to do so, the only way to preserve society is to try to
simulate the effects of goodwill by writing thousands of nit-picking
rules.

Dale

