   There is only one way to properly define the big basket of groups that
   are libertarian -- they are people who oppose the initiation of
   non-concentual violent force against persons or their property, or the
   credible threat of such initiation. Thats what libertarians are. Thats
   the only definition you get to go by. Another, less clean-cut way to
   put it is that libertarians are people who want all relationships to
   be based on mutual consent.

However, there are subtleties to this -- using physical force to keep
someone off my property is not considered "initiating force", etc.  So
buried within the concept of "initiation of force" is the concept of
property, life, and the protection thereof.  I think those need a bit
more exposition.

   Somehow, if you put two libertarians into isolated rooms and ask them
   the libertarian position on a novel issue, they usually both manage to
   parrot the same answer. Libertarianism may have its flaws, but a lack
   of internal consistancy and clarity of stance is not one of them. That
   tends to mean to me that either you do not fully understand
   libertarianism, or,that you do understand it and that you are simply
   baiting us.

Well, what you've mentioned neither proves internal consistency nor
clairity, unfortunately.  All they prove is that all libertarians
believe the same cluster of ideas, but not that those ideas are
internally consistent or that they are clearly exposited to outsiders.
(Consider the Scholastic philosophy of the late middle ages:  It had a
set answer to almost any question, and it was internally consistent,
within its own rules of reasoning.  But it was not really internally
consistent, and it was hardly clear.)

In addition, this apparent consistency is mostly due to people not
asking the right questions.  Try asking something that strikes at the
heart of the theories of property and rights, such as "How long should
copyright protection last?" and the consistency will be much less.

But all those considerations are actually beside the point.  I suspect
that the consistency of libertarians' opinions is due to libertarians
being people of a very similar mindset.  (If you'll forgive me: INTx
in the Meyers-Briggs system.)  It's not too surprising that if you
select a large group of people with similar personalities, that they
will prefer similar political systems.

   However, we seem to have an answer to the question, since you yourself
